  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nBy midday Monday, medium range models are in fairly good \nagreement, bringing a strong upper level trough into southern \nCalifornia, while at the same time, Hurricane Polo is expected to \nbe turning northeast and headed toward the Baja Peninsula. Out \nahead of the upper level low, surface winds will increase out of \nthe south/southeast, pulling Gulf moisture into west Texas. The \nupper level trough is expected pull the remnants of Hurricane Polo\nnortheastward into northwestern Mexico Monday night into Tuesday.\nThe combination of lift from the upper level trough and the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo, along with plentiful moisture \navailable is still expected to bring a good chance for \nprecipitation to west and west-central Texas from Tuesday into the\nsecond half of next week. The most favorable time period will \nlikely be from Tuesday night into Wednesday night as a Pacific \ncold front moves into the area and combines with the upper level \nsupport. While the highest potential for heavy rainfall lies to \nthe west of our area, there will be the potential for heavy \nrainfall and flooding in our area as well. In addition to the \nprecipitation chances, temperatures should cool from highs in the \n90s down into the 80s through the end of next week.",
